Audit-only headers for scripts/audit-libc16 (docs/M3-RUNTIME-AUDIT.md).

They stand in for headers that libc16 does not have, so that every module
of the interpreter can be compiled against libc16's headers and the
symbols it would then need can be listed. They are never used to build
PERL.EXE, and they are not a design for the eventual headers: each one
declares only what the Perl 3.044 sources and perl16's own files use,
with the types ISO C (C89) or POSIX give them, in Open Watcom's default
register convention. A symbol declared here therefore appears in the
audit as NAME_ (not libc16's __cdecl _NAME), which is how the audit
tells "libc16 lacks this" from "libc16 provides this".

audit.h is force-included (wcc -fi) and supplies the few macros that
libc16's own headers lack (errno values, stdaux/stdprn).

Sources: ISO/IEC 9899:1990 clauses 7.5, 7.6 and 7.7; POSIX.1-1990 for
the errno names; the Microsoft C 5.1 interface for process.h's P_WAIT
and spawn/exec names. Nothing here was taken from Open Watcom's or any
other runtime's headers.
